Potential issue: On the hilt step (8 options), the option list scrolls; when the user selects an option lower down, renderOptions re-renders and may reset scroll to top? The innerHTML replacement resets the scroll position of the option list to 0, so after selecting an option, the list jumps to top. That could be annoying. The previous code re-rendered the whole option list on every selection. Now that it scrolls, selecting an option lower down would reset scroll. We should preserve the scroll position when re-rendering. This is a real usability issue introduced by making the list scrollable. We can fix by saving `const scrollTop = $('optionList').scrollTop` before innerHTML and restoring after. In renderOptions, capture scrollTop at start, then after setting innerHTML and event listeners, set `$('optionList').scrollTop = scrollTop`. But when changing steps, we want scroll top reset to 0. We can reset on setStep. Or simply preserve scroll on option selection and reset when step changes.

Currently `renderOptions` is called from setStep and from option click. We can modify:
- At start of renderOptions: `const previousScroll = $('optionList').scrollTop;`
- At end: `$('optionList').scrollTop = previousScroll;`
But when setStep changes step, previousScroll might carry over from previous step, which could be odd. Add `$('optionList').scrollTop = 0;` in setStep before renderOptions or after. Alternatively use a flag. Simpler: in option click handler, before calling renderOptions, save scroll; but renderOptions rebuilds. We can capture and restore within the click handler:
```
const listScroll = $('optionList').scrollTop;
renderOptions();
$('optionList').scrollTop = listScroll;
```
But renderOptions is called at the end of the click handler, then updateUI etc. We can modify the handler:
```
const scrollTop = $('optionList').scrollTop;
selected...; ...
renderOptions(); updateUI(); ...
$('optionList').scrollTop = scrollTop;
```
This preserves scroll when clicking an option. When setStep calls renderOptions, it resets naturally to 0 because innerHTML replacement. But wait, the option list scrollTop might persist across innerHTML? Replacing innerHTML resets scrollTop to 0. So setStep resets automatically. Good. Let's add scroll preservation to the click handler. This is a nice polish.

Also the step list scroll: when setStep changes, renderSteps re-renders the step list innerHTML, resetting scroll to top. If the user clicks step 9 after scrolling, it will jump to top, hiding the active step. That's also annoying. We should preserve or ensure active step visible. Since renderSteps re-renders, we can save scrollTop and restore. But if the user clicks step 9, the active step is step 9 and the list should scroll to show it. Preserving scroll top would show step 9 if they had scrolled to click it. If they click a step via keyboard (ArrowRight) while the list is scrolled, preserving scroll may hide the new active step. Better to ensure active step is visible after render. Could implement:
```
const activeButton = document.querySelector('.step-item.active');
if (activeButton) activeButton.scrollIntoView({block:'nearest'});
```
after render. But `scrollIntoView` might scroll the whole page; use `block:'nearest'` within the scroll container. It could also scroll the window if needed. Since the list is scrollable, it should scroll the list. This would make the active step visible. Good. But calling scrollIntoView on every render may cause the left rail to jump when selecting options? renderSteps is only called on setStep, not option selection. So fine.

For the option list, after selecting an option, re-render resets scroll. Preserve. Let's implement both.

In `renderSteps`, after event listeners, add:
```
const activeItem = document.querySelector('.step-item.active');
activeItem?.scrollIntoView({ block: 'nearest' });
```
But this might scroll the page vertically if the list is not scrollable? `block:'nearest'` should not scroll if already visible. Fine. Alternatively set `$('stepList').scrollTop` to active item offset. But scrollIntoView is simpler. Note: `scrollIntoView` on page load may scroll the app-shell? The active step 1 is visible, no scroll. Fine.

In option click handler, capture scroll:
```
const optionList = $('optionList');
const scrollTop = optionList.scrollTop;
selected[...] = ...
...
renderOptions(); optionList.scrollTop = scrollTop; updateUI(); ...
```
But `optionList` is the same DOM element; innerHTML replaced, scrollTop may reset. Set after renderOptions. Good.

